Title: Induction Motor Fault Diagnosis Using Infrared Thermography

CNN Prediction:
- Predicted fault: Overheating
- Confidence: 0.803

Observed Evidence:
No textual thermal evidence was produced by the CNN; only the predicted class and confidence are available.

Retrieved Technical Knowledge:
[Context 1]
Fault Type: Overheating
Section: full_entry
Retrieval score: 0.3808
Fault Type: Overheating
Aliases: thermal overload; excessive temperature rise; abnormal heating; temperature rise; hot motor
Description: Overheating represents an abnormal thermal condition in which the motor operates above its expected thermal behavior for the applied load, ambient condition, duty cycle, and cooling configuration. In induction motors, this condition is critical because temperature rise is directly associated with accelerated insulation aging, winding degradation, reduction of dielectric strength, and increased probability of premature failure. Physical Mechanism: The fault is usually produced by an imbalance between heat generation and heat dissipation. Heat generation may increase due to overload, voltage imbalance, repeated starting, high current, winding defects, poor power quality, or mechanical friction. Heat dissipation may decrease due to obstructed ventilation, dirty cooling channels, damaged fans, high ambient temperature, or inappropriate installation clearance. Root Causes: Operation above rated load or service factor; Restricted airflow or blocked ventilation ducts;

Supporting Evidence:
